<h1>Configuring a run</h1>
<p>Every value a test exposes in the controller's run panel is a
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/configuration-fields/">config field</a> on its config class.
  A subclass that redeclares a field with a new default is a configured copy of the test, and the
  framework treats the copy exactly like the original. The controller's own CI export uses this
  mechanism for its mirror classes. The AI agent uses it in the same way.</p>

<h2 id="configured-copy">Configured copy</h2>
<p>For the request "Run the TCP Traffic group: Linux machines as fan-out, Windows machines as link
  partners", the AI agent writes this file into the run folder:</p>

{{ codeblock.block('from orcraft import OrcraftConfigField
from traffic_lib.base_traffic_test import DirectionalTrafficConfig
from test_group_tcp.test_tcp_tx.test_tcp_tx import TestTcpTx


class DirectionalTrafficConfig(DirectionalTrafficConfig):
	sut_endpoint = OrcraftConfigField.EndpointFanout(
		default=("lab-linux-1", "lab-linux-2"),
		label="SUT Endpoint",
	)
	link_partner_endpoints = OrcraftConfigField.MultiSelectEndpoints(
		default=("lab-win-1", "lab-win-2"),
		label="Link Partner Endpoints",
	)
	nuttcp_tool_package = OrcraftConfigField.PackageFolder(
		default="/home/lab/orcraft/tools/nuttcp",
		label="Nuttcp Tool Package",
	)


class TestTcpTx(TestTcpTx):
	def __init__(self, config=None):
		super().__init__(config if config is not None else DirectionalTrafficConfig(name="TCP Transmit"))') }}

<p>What changed against the original:</p>
<ul>
  <li>The SUT field was redeclared from a single <code>Endpoint</code> to an
    <code>EndpointFanout</code>. The test now runs once per machine listed, in parallel.</li>
  <li>The link partner field kept its <code>MultiSelectEndpoints</code> type and received the partner
    machines. Every run shares them.</li>
  <li>The tool package, a required field with no value in the code, was filled in. A standalone run has
    no run panel to ask for it.</li>
</ul>
<p>Everything else is inherited: the test body, the other fields and the result fields. The subclasses
  keep the names of the classes they extend, as the CI export's mirrors do, so the file reads as the
  same test with its values filled in. The endpoint field types are described on the
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/endpoints/">Endpoints</a> page.</p>

<div class="docs-callout">
  <span class="docs-callout__label">Run folder</span>
  <p>A run file is configuration for one run, not code. It lives in a folder of its own beside the suite
    repositories, never inside one, and the test it configures is never edited. The batch on the
    <a class="text-accent" href="/ai-agents/running/">Running a batch</a> page tells the framework
    where the repository is, so the imports above resolve without the repository being touched.</p>
</div>

<h2 id="containers">Groups, suites and batches</h2>
<p>A group or a suite is configured the same way, through a subclass of its own config class. Whatever
  the request covers, one item or several, the configured copies are listed in a
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/batches/">batch</a> in the same run folder. The batch is what
  runs them, as the <a class="text-accent" href="/ai-agents/running/">Running a batch</a> page
  shows.</p>
